[03:32.98]4. What is the hottest exercise for the girls in the gym£¿
[03:52.04]Conversation Two
[03:54.10]W: Welcome to our TV show, Book Club. 
[03:57.25]Today we are honored to have Professor David here to introduce his new book¡ªThe Unconscious Mind.
[04:04.66]M: Thank you. The subtitle of my book is How Your Unconscious Mind Rules Your Behavior. 
[04:10.65]So you may have a clue about my book. 
[04:13.92]By the way, have you read any books written by a psychologist£¿
[04:18.46]W: When I was at college, I read Freud out of curiosity. 
[04:22.73]I was deeply attracted by his ideas about the unconscious¡ªa sort of shadowy basement of the mind that is inaccessible to rational thought, but which nevertheless influences people's behavior.
[04:35.66]M: You are right. 
[04:37.29]That is the core of Freud's theory about the unconscious. 
[04:40.87]Although it remained popular at dinner parties, the idea of the unconscious fell out of favor among 20th-century psychologists, thanks to the rise of more scientific approaches to psychology.
[04:53.91]W: How about your latest book£¿ 
[04:55.77]Does it share similar ideas with modern psychology£¿
[04:59.36]M: My book shows how the idea of the unconscious has become respectable again over the past couple of decades.
[05:06.24]W: Why£¿
[05:07.71]M: This development has been helped by experimental evidence of the effects of the subconscious and, especially, by real-time brain-scanning technology. 
[05:17.33]It allows researchers to examine what is going on in their subjects' heads.
[05:22.07]W: It sounds quite convincing.
[05:24.49]M: Yes. The experimental evidence suggests that, as Freud suspected, conscious reasoning makes up a small part of the activity in our brains, with most of the work taking place where we can't tap into it.
[05:38.17]W: So your book supports Freud's theory completely.
[05:41.85]M: Not 100% though. 
[05:43.83]Unlike Freud's unconscious, the modern unconscious is a place of super-fast data processing, useful survival mechanisms and rules about the world that have been around for millions of years of evolution.
[05:56.59]W: That is a new idea indeed.
[05:59.36]M: The well-documented tendency of humans to categorize almost every piece of information they come across is a survival mechanism that evolved to aid quick decision making. 
[06:09.97]This is the essence of my book.
[06:14.10]Questions 5 to 8 are based on the conversation you have just heard.
[06:19.06]5. Why did the woman read Freud when she was a college student£¿
[06:38.05]6. Why was the idea of the unconscious popular again over the past years£¿
[06:58.42]7. What can we learn from the experimental evidence£¿
[07:17.05]8. According to the man, which is the key point of his book£¿
