[08:05.88]Passage One
[08:07.79]Smartwatches are designed to keep us from being glued to our smartphone screens all day. 
[08:14.11]But are these devices distracting for use behind the wheel£¿ 
[08:18.75]Some other countries' police officers certainly seem to think so. 
[08:23.45]A Canadian man was fined $120 for using his smartwatch while driving earlier this week. 
[08:31.36]That raises a lot of questions about how smartwatches¡ªand future wearable devices¡ªfit into a growing number of state¡°distracted driving¡±laws that aim to ban texting, some phone calls and other electronic device use while driving.
[08:48.89]From a legal standpoint, it's hard to say whether smartwatches automatically get you in trouble.
[08:55.64] The Canadian law prohibits use of a¡°hand-held¡±device, which raises questions about whether smartwatches fall into the category. 
[09:04.96]You aren't, after all, holding a smartwatch when you use it. 
[09:08.94]It's on your wrist.
[09:10.34]But safety¡ªnot the fear of a ticket¡ªshould be the main concern for tech-loving drivers, said road safety advocates.
[09:18.83]¡°The technology is advancing faster than the laws can keep up, in many cases,¡±said Kara Macek, director of communications for the Governors Highway Safety Association.
[09:31.90]According to a survey, 660,000 US drivers were using phones or other electronics while driving last year. 
[09:41.45]And, the average text takes your eyes off the road for five seconds, which at highway speeds will take you the length of a football field.
[09:50.86]So, it is recommended that drivers should put their smartphones, and smartwatches as well, aside while driving.
[10:00.77]Questions 9 to 11 are based on the passage you have just heard.
[10:05.86]9. What happened to a Canadian man earlier this week£¿
[10:23.91]10. Why is banning the use of smartwatch while driving in dispute in Canada£¿
[10:44.93]11. What do road safety advocates suggest tech-loving drivers do£¿
[11:03.80]Passage Two
[11:05.51]Many young people struggle to get a good night's sleep which could be harming their health, according to the results of a major survey published to mark World Sleep Day.
[11:15.38]The Sleep Research Society surveyed nearly 60,000 people aged 10 to 45, and found that 91% of them reported¡°not having enough sleep¡±or¡°still feeling tired after sleep¡±. 
[11:28.39]Only about 5.6% of respondents said they felt energetic after sleep. 
[11:33.78]About three quarters of respondents said they experienced difficulty falling asleep, and 13% said they really struggled to fall asleep. 
[11:42.42]Only about 11% said they could sleep through the night without waking up.
[11:47.28]Many respondents cited work pressure as a major influence on their sleep quality, and could lead to bad sleep.
[11:54.56]The survey showed 60% of the respondents would choose to work instead of sleep.
[12:00.21]In general, people with good education backgrounds tended to be more self-disciplined about getting enough sleep, but only 5% of respondents had a routine daily schedule.
[12:11.66]Sleep problems have little direct link to income, working conditions or social status, according to the survey.
[12:19.75]¡°The portion of people with bad sleep is almost the same in different social layers,¡± the report stated.
[12:26.11]Surveyors said that stress, anxiety, and low spirits were the main culprits in bad sleep, worsened by the obsession with electronic products that could cause blue light effects disrupting sleep.
[12:39.02]Some 93% said they would play with smartphones before sleep, watching TV series or shopping online.
[12:47.07]Surveyors believed that the random life schedules would lead young people to have low work efficiency, low appetite but higher cancer risk.
[12:56.83]Questions 12 to 15 are based on the passage you have just heard.
[13:02.25]12. What did the Sleep Research Society find through their survey£¿
[13:22.30]13. What kind of people tended to be more self-disciplined about getting enough sleep according to the survey£¿
[13:44.71]14. Which was a main cause of bad sleep according to the surveyors£¿
[14:04.71]15. What may be the result of random life schedules for young people£¿
