[14:52.89]Recording One
[14:55.43]Let's say you hear people chewing gum or clicking pens. 
[14:59.61]Just for fun, try it now. 
[15:02.32]How do you feel£¿
[15:03.82]Well, UCL scientists have found that people who find the sounds of chewing gum or pen clicking unbearable have a genuine brain abnormality.
[15:14.39]While many people find hearing people eat annoying or pen clicking troubling, others who suffer from misophonia report feeling disgust when exposed to the noises.
[15:25.85]Referring to¡°trigger sounds¡±, people with misophonia can respond with an intense ¡°fight or flight¡±reaction.
[15:33.40]Now researchers at Newcastle University have reported finding a difference in the frontal lobe in misophonia sufferers, suggesting it is a genuine condition where medical opinion in the past has been skeptical.
[15:47.31]Writing in the journal Current Biology, they found changes in the brain activity when a trigger sound is experienced.
[15:55.23]They also found people with misophonia experienced an increased heart rate and sweated when they were confronted by a trigger sound.
[16:03.77]Researchers found a difference in the¡°emotional control mechanism¡±that causes their brains to go into overdrive on hearing trigger sounds.
[16:12.75]Dr. Sukhbinder Kumar, from the Institute of Neuroscience at Newcastle University, said, ¡°For many people with misophonia, this will come as welcome news as for the first time we have demonstrated a difference in brain structure and function in sufferers. This study demonstrates the critical brain changes as further evidence to convince a skeptical medical community that this is a genuine disorder.¡±
[16:41.24]Tim Griffiths, Professor of Cognitive Neurology at Newcastle University and UCL, said, ¡°I hope this will reassure sufferers. I was part of the skeptical community myself until we saw patients in the clinic and understood how strikingly similar the features are.¡±
[17:00.27]One misophonia sufferer said her GP laughed when she told him about her symptoms.
[17:05.96]Olana Tansley-Hancock, 29, from Ashford in Kent, was eight years old when family meals became unbearable for her.
[17:14.42]She said,¡°The noise of my family eating forced me to retreat to my own bedroom for meals.¡±
[17:20.87]The issue came to a head when she went to university and had to move train carriages seven times because the noise of people eating and rustling papers was unbearable.
[17:31.67]¡°When I saw my GP at the time, he laughed at me,¡±she said.
[17:35.68]¡°Then I tried a counsellor but in my case, that made it worse as it made me even more sensitive to sound.¡±
[17:42.92]After researching misophonia, she has changed her lifestyle, reducing her caffeine and alcohol intake and using headphones when visiting the cinema.
[17:53.02]She said,¡°This research is a huge relief as it shows there is a physical basis for misophonia which should help others understand the condition. It also opens up the opportunity for better management.¡±
[18:08.88]Questions 16 to 18 are based on the recording you have just heard.
