[19:11.48]Recording Two
[19:13.71]An end to grey hair and crows feet could be just 10 years away after scientists showed it is possible to reverse aging in animals.
[19:22.72]Using a new technique which takes adult cells back to their embryonic form, US researchers at the Salk Institute in California, showed it was possible to reverse aging in mice, allowing the animals to not only look younger, but live for 30% longer.
[19:39.52]The technique involves stimulating four genes which are particularly active during development in the womb. 
[19:45.86]It was also found to work to turn the clock back on human skin cells in the lab, making them look and behave younger.
[19:53.41]Scientists hope to eventually create a drug which can mimic the effect of the found genes which could be taken to slow down, and even reverse the aging process. 
[20:04.46]They say it will take around 10 years to get to human trials.
[20:08.29]¡°Obviously, mice are not humans and we know it will be much more complex to rejuvenate a person. But this study shows that aging is a very dynamic and plastic process,¡± said Dr. Juan Carlos Izpisua Belmonte, a professor in Salk's Gene Expression Laboratory.
[20:26.94]Scientists have known for some time that the four genes, which are known collectively as the Yamanaka factors, could turn adult cells back to their stem cell state, where they can grow into any part of the body. 
[20:40.50]But it was always feared that allowing that to happen could damage organs made from the cells, and even trigger cancer.
[20:47.98]However, it was discovered that stimulating the genes intermittently reversed aging, without causing any damaging side effects.
[20:56.78]In mice with a premature aging disease, the treatment countered signs of aging and increased their lifespan by 30%. 
[21:04.97]If it worked similarly in humans it could allow people to live until more than 100 years old. 
[21:11.17]In healthy mice it also helped damaged organs to heal faster.
[21:17.21]Questions 19 to 21 are based on the recording you have just heard.
[21:22.56]19. What did the research carried out by US researchers at the Salk Institute in California show£¿
[21:46.74]20. What can we know about the new technique£¿
[22:04.91]21. What is the potential risk of turning adult cells back to their stem cell state£¿
[22:26.23]Recording Three
[22:28.32]Scans of 25 first-time moms showed these structural brain changes lasted for at least two years after giving birth. 
[22:37.13]European researchers said the scale of brain changes during pregnancy were similar to those seen during adolescence.
[22:44.45]But they found no evidence of women's memory deteriorating.
[22:49.04]Many women have said that they feel forgetful and emotional during pregnancy and put it down to¡°pregnancy¡±or¡°baby¡±brain¡ªand, it seems, with good reason.
[22:59.85]Pregnancy is characterized by extreme rise of sex hormones and involves drastic physiological and physical changes in the body, the researchers say.
[23:10.32]During those nine months, women experience a flood of estrogen which is greater than for the whole of the rest of their lives.
[23:19.00]Yet research on the effects of pregnancy on the human brain is scarce.
[23:23.52]This study, from researchers at the Universitat Autonoma de Barcelona and Leiden University and published in Nature Neuroscience, looked at the brain scans of women before they became pregnant, soon after they gave birth, and two years later, to see how the brain changed.
[23:42.20]And they compared these women's brains with those of 19 first-time fathers, 17 men without children and 20 women who had never given birth. 
[23:52.45]The researchers found substantial reductions in the volume of grey matter in the brains of first-time mothers.
[23:59.88]The grey matter changes occurred in areas of the brain involved in social interactions used for attributing thoughts and feelings to other people¡ªknown as¡°theory-of-mind¡±tasks.
[24:11.90]The researchers thought this would give new mothers an advantage in various ways¡ªhelp them recognize the needs of their children, be more aware of potential social threats and become more attached to their babies.
[24:27.18]Questions 22 to 25 are based on the recording you have just heard.
